or ResumeCurriculum Vitae of Daniel Pfeifer.pdfPersonal Head
Shot/Photographdpfeifer_fit.pdfShort BiographyProf. Pfeifer teaches Applied
Computer Science in the Medical Informatics program of Heilbronn and
Heidelberg Universities. He has a strong background in data processing and
software engineering. In recent years he put focus on developing
Metagenomics software in particular to advance and simplify the detection
of pathogens.=3D=3D Abstract Information =3D=3D

Topic TitleMetagenomics for Pathogen DetectionBackgroundDetecting
pathogenic infections in animals and humans is of crucial importance for
the appropriate therapy. Current types of laboratory tests rely either on
immunological host response or PCR. However, immunologically based tests
may fail even in the presence of a corresponding pathogen =E2=80=93 if the =
immune
response is defective, suppressed, or in the case of an antibody test, if
appropriate antibodies do not fit the test's antigen. PCR tests are used
for highly sensitive, DNA-based tests: they rely on a so-called primer =E2=
=80=93
a short DNA sequence which uniquely belongs to the genus or species of a
pathogen in question and must be chosen prior to testing. Nonetheless, PCR
based tests fail if a pathogen other than the one for which the primer was
designed causes disease or if too little respective DNA is in the sample.

In contrast to the PCR, metagenomics offers the chance of finding
pathogens' DNA without a primer as it considers all of the pathogens' DNA
at the same time. Moreover, very many (if not all) potential pathogens are
checked together in a single test! In metagenomics, simply all DNA
fragments from a sample that pass the sequencing device are sequenced and
the data from the fragments is written to a potentially very large file (a
so-called fastq file). Therefore, the procedure analyzes host DNA but also
pathogens=E2=80=99 DNA if present. In a second step, the DNA fragments stor=
ed in
the fastq file are checked against the reference genomes of all pathogens
in question. This a computationally complex IT-based process but thanks to
improvements in hardware and analysis software, it can be performed on
regular computers and PCs.

The result of the IT processing is a statistical report with numbers of DNA
matches for each pathogen (in terms of so-called k-mers). High counts of
respective hits may indicate that (remains of) respective pathogens were
present in the original sample. Experience shows that using Metagenomics,
pathogens can usually be identified down to the species level or beyond and
that different types of pathogens are indeed found if they were also
present in the sample. Several publications using this technology report
that its sensitivity is comparable or even slightly superior to PCR tests.

As sequencing devices have become more affordable and compact, the
technology becomes attractive for even small-scale laboratories.AimThe main
aim of this presentation is to give the audience a basic understanding of
Metagenomics as a new technology for pathogen detection. It highlights its
basics in bio informatics and explains the overall processing steps in
metagenomics. 

The presentation explains the IT processing chain required to determine
hits for pathogens. The author has expert knowledge with regard to IT
processing for metagenomics as he has developed a highly accurate and
efficient software system for this very purpose.

The presentation covers recent scientific publications where metagenomics
was successfully applied to detect tick-borne infections in ticks, dogs and
deer based on inexpensive sequencing devices. We show how respective
results for DNA matches can be reproduced easily via user-friendly software
on the basis of raw DNA sequencing files.

The presentation further details metagenomics for pathogen detection via
three experiments conducted by the author and co-researchers: 
=E2=80=A2 Software based analysis results are shown based on raw sequencing=
 files
from 8 field-collected ticks. Some major tick-borne infections were
discovered. 
=E2=80=A2 Software based analysis results are shown based on raw sequencing=
 files
from 15 human saliva samples to find typical human viruses often occurring
in the mouth. 
=E2=80=A2 20 soil samples were sequenced to detect fungi which are harmful =
to
certain plants in soil using Nanopore sequencing. Results for one fungus
were confirmed with a PCR test.

More generally, the three experiments aim to show the broad use of
Metagenomics in various fields such as ecological surveillance, medicine,
and agriculture.Patients and MethodsAs mentioned in =E2=80=9CAims=E2=80=9D =
the
presentation covers three experiments:
1) For a small study, we received the raw genomic data files from 10 humans
who had their genome analyzed via =E2=80=9CDante Labs=E2=80=9D (an online s=
ervice for
personalized whole genome sequencing). The sequencing was based on saliva
samples. The data owners gave consent to the analysis prior to the
experiment. We analyzed the raw data files with respect to DNA fragments
from human viruses using metagenomics software (named =E2=80=9CGenestrip=E2=
=80=9D). Our
analysis database covers 750 genomes of most to all viruses and virus
variants that are currently known to be potentially pathogenic to humans
(to be inspected online via 
https://genestrip.it.hs-heilbronn.de/gweb/dbinfo?lan=3Den&dbid=3D5
. We checked respective hit counts and statistical results for consistency
and checked whether strong hits aligned with general medical expectations
for viruses in the mouth.
2) Regarding bacterial tick-borne infections, we used the raw analysis
files from the Nature publication =E2=80=9CMetagenomic surveillance for bac=
terial
tick-borne pathogens using nanopore adaptive sampling=E2=80=9D and processe=
d them
with our metagenomics software. (The original raw data files are available
in fastq format for download at the =E2=80=9CNational Center for Biotechnol=
ogy
Information=E2=80=9D in the US.) Eight field-collected ticks (species I.
scapularis) from different locations in the US were used for sequencing. We
compared the metagenomic analysis results of our software with those
reported in the above publication. Our analysis database covers over 700
genomes of potentially tick-borne bacterial pathogens (to be inspected
online via 
https://genestrip.it.hs-heilbronn.de/gweb/dbinfo?lan=3Den&dbid=3D4
) including various species and strains of Yersinia, Bartonella,
Rickettsia, Anaplasma, Ehrlichia, Borrelia, Borreliella, Chlamydia and
Mycoplasma.
3) We applied metagenomics to check whether certain fungi were present in
twenty soil samples from around Heilbronn =E2=80=93 a German city known for
growing wine. All of the ten fungi species tested are harmful to grape vine
plants. The analysis database can be inspected online via
https://genestrip.it.hs-heilbronn.de/gweb/dbinfo?lan=3Den&dbid=3D8
. 
We used a Nanopore MinION as a sequencing device and a DNA extraction
toolkit from Qiagen suitable for soil (DNeasy PowerSoil Pro Kit). We
followed the protocols from Qiagen and Nanopore for sample preparation. For
partial control, the samples were tested against one fungus (=E2=80=9CBotry=
tis
cinerea=E2=80=9D) via PCR with two samples being positive. (This fungus was=
 also
included in the metagenomic analysis.) 
Experiment 3) was lead by Dr. Robert Friedrich and conducted in cooperation
with the Biolab of the experimenta Science Center in
Heilbronn.ResultsRegarding the three experiments:
1) There were high numbers of k-mers for Human betaherpesvirus 7 in all 10
samples. As an example, consider =E2=80=9Cperson 3=E2=80=9C: We found Human
betaherpesvirus 7 (with 448890 k-mers / 108664 unique k-mers) accounting
for more than 78% of the viral genome. Note that k-mers represent unique
DNA fragments from the respective pathogen=E2=80=99s genome, each consistin=
g of
exactly 31 base pairs. The total number of k-mers in the corresponding two
fastq files of person 3 was 297412346832, which corresponds to almost 200
GBs of compressed data on disk. In this case, the software-based analysis
took 173 minutes on a modern Mac and computer memory consumption was less
than 4 GB.
In addition, we found high numbers for Human gammaherpesvirus 4 (EBV) in
the sample from person 5 (with 4061786 k-mers / 4151610 unique k-mers) but
not in samples from any persons. Person 5 had fatigue and other symptoms
consistent with a potentially chronic activation of EBV. The k-mers found
there, cover more than 86% of the EBV genome.
Results for other viruses were inconclusive or k-mers were too low to be
considered hits.
2) All of the results from the mentioned publication could be recovered and
confirmed. As an example, consider =E2=80=9Ctick 3=E2=80=9D: We found Borre=
lia
miyamotoi species (with 95998 k-mers / 92786 unique k-mers), Borreliella
burgdorferi species (with 11330 k-mers / 11127 unique k-mers), Anaplasma
phagocytophilum species (with 6253 k-mers / 3099 unique k-mers) and
Rickettsia genus (with 7803 k-mers / 7796 unique k-mers). The total number
of k-mers in the corresponding fastq file was 214436633. The above numbers
constitute very good hits because of high values and a consistent ratio
between k-mers and unique k-mers. Analysis time per fastq file was less
than 2 minutes and computer memory consumption for analysis was less than
16 GB. Here is the direct online link to our results for tick 3:
https://genestrip.it.hs-heilbronn.de/gweb/view?lan=3Den&jobid=3D35
3) We found increased k-mers and unique k-mers for Botrytis cinerea in just
the samples where the PCR for Botrytis cinerea was positive. (Further
details on this will be included in the presentation.)Conclusions or
DiscussionExperiment 1) shows that metagenomics can be used to find viruses
residing in the mouth. The samples confirmed the presence of Human
betaherpesvirus 7 in each of the individuals. This is impressive when
considering that the actual sequencing data was not even gathered for the
purpose of pathogen detection but for the analysis of the person=E2=80=99s
(human) genome instead. Human betaherpesvirus 7 has a prevalence rate
greater than 85%, which is in line with the results for our samples. It
resides in saliva glands which also matches our type samples.
The hit for the EBV in the case of person 5 was indicative of an active EBV
infection and matched the person=E2=80=99s overall symptoms.

Experiment 2) confirmed the results of a Nature publication that analyzed
bacterial pathogens in field-collected ticks. Our software analysis on the
raw data files was perfectly consistent with the findings from the
publication. This partly confirms the accuracy of the employed software but
more importantly, it confirms that metagenomics would be an appropriate
tool for tick surveillance. The results are suggestive for applying this
technology to ticks collected after a tick bite: by taking a related future
test, a person bitten by a tick could be informed about the tick=E2=80=99s
pathogens and could take appropriate actions to prevent potential disease
development.

Experiment 3) suggests that Metagenomics can potentially be used in
agriculture to detect fungal infections and to predict or prevent an
outbreak in vineyards, for example. We mainly chose the example to gain
experience with sequencing and analysis tools without (already) having to
deal with human samples or samples potentially dangerous to humans. The
device that we used is small and relatively easy to handle. Experiment 3)
also allowed for assessing the total cost for a potential future test (i.e.
for a =E2=80=9Ctick test=E2=80=9D as mentioned above): We used an inexpensi=
ve
sequencing device which was also used in the above-mentioned publication
(worth 2,000 USD). Associated consumable materials per test are worth less
than 100 USD. The staff hours for a future test are high as it takes
approximately one work day to prepare a respective sample for sequencing.
However, by using an approach called multiplexing, about 24 samples can be
jointly prepared and sequenced, which may greatly reduce staff hours per
sample. The cost and time for the up-following IT-based analysis is
negligible.What are the scientific implications of your presentation?The
few existing publications on metagenomics for pathogen detection mainly
focus on infections in animals. In this context, metagenomics has been
successfully applied to detect blood-borne chronic infections including
Mycoplasma, Bartonella, Babesia and types of Filaria. Borrelia has also
been successfully detected in ticks via metagenomics. The pathogens can
usually be identified down to the species level and beyond. Respective
publications report that the sensitivity of metagenomics is comparable to
PCR if not better.
The authors=E2=80=99 experience and findings as presented via three experim=
ents
are in line with the findings from the above-mentioned scientific
publications.
Metagenomics is becoming a standard tool in various fields of science, in
particular biology and ecology, as well as in research on veterinary
medicine.What are the clinical implications of your presentation?The
results of the presentation and related publications show that metagenomics
is on the verge of becoming an important diagnostic tool. Indeed, there
already exist respective lab tests from startup companies / labs. Some US
hospitals use metagenomic analysis to determine sepsis pathogens accurately
and quickly for the best treatment outcome.

When metagenomics becomes broadly available for diagnostics, physicians
will not have to make as many upfront guesses on infections present in
order to choose an appropriate test as metagenomics can cover many
pathogens in a single test. Physicians and laboratory staff will have to
consider and integrate an IT based analysis step into the whole testing
procedure. This differs from conventional diagnostic tests that do not
demand much IT processing.

As metagenomics returns statistics on DNA-based hits for pathogens,
physicians will need training to read, understand and interpret these
statistics correctly for appropriate conclusions and therapeutic steps.

As the cost for metagenomic analysis has decreased substantially, it has
become attractive as an option to replace established laboratory tests for
pathogens.Has this information been presented before?YesIf yes, please tell
us where?It has been presented in parts at the annual meeting of the
"Deutsche Borreliose Gesellschaft" (German Lyme Disease Association) in
Dec. 2024.Relevant Scientific References"Metagenomic surveillance for
bacterial tick-borne pathogens using nanopore adaptive sampling"
https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-023-37134-9
(Nature, 2023)

