l, sans-serif;">Background</th><td valign=3D"top" style=3D"-webkit-text-siz=
e-adjust: 100%;-ms-text-size-adjust: 100%;mso-table-lspace: 0pt;mso-table-r=
space: 0pt;text-align:left;padding-top: 15px;padding-bottom: 15px;border-bo=
ttom: 1px solid #eeeeee;font-family: Helvetica, Arial, sans-serif;">Detecti=
ng pathogenic infections in animals and humans is of crucial importance for=
 the appropriate therapy. Current types of laboratory tests rely either on =
immunological host response or PCR. However, immunologically based tests ma=
y fail even in the presence of a corresponding pathogen =E2=80=93 if the im=
mune response is defective, suppressed, or in the case of an antibody test,=
 if appropriate antibodies do not fit the test's antigen. PCR tests are use=
d for highly sensitive, DNA-based tests: they rely on a so-called primer =
=E2=80=93 a short DNA sequence which uniquely belongs to the genus or speci=
es of a pathogen in question and must be chosen prior to testing. Nonethele=
ss, PCR based tests fail if a pathogen other than the one for which the pri=
mer was designed causes disease or if too little respective DNA is in the s=
ample.<br><br>In contrast to the PCR, metagenomics offers the chance of fin=
ding pathogens' DNA without a primer as it considers all of the pathogens' =
DNA at the same time. Moreover, very many (if not all) potential pathogens =
are checked together in a single test! In metagenomics, simply all DNA frag=
ments from a sample that pass the sequencing device are sequenced and the d=
ata from the fragments is written to a potentially very large file (a so-ca=
lled fastq file). Therefore, the procedure analyzes host DNA but also patho=
gens=E2=80=99 DNA if present. In a second step, the DNA fragments stored in=
 the fastq file are checked against the reference genomes of all pathogens =
in question. This a computationally complex IT-based process but thanks to =
improvements in hardware and analysis software, it can be performed on regu=
lar computers and PCs.<br><br>The result of the IT processing is a statisti=
cal report with numbers of DNA matches for each pathogen (in terms of so-ca=
lled k-mers). High counts of respective hits may indicate that (remains of)=
 respective pathogens were present in the original sample. Experience shows=
 that using Metagenomics, pathogens can usually be identified down to the s=
pecies level or beyond and that different types of pathogens are indeed fou=
nd if they were also present in the sample. Several publications using this=
 technology report that its sensitivity is comparable or even slightly supe=
rior to PCR tests.<br><br>As sequencing devices have become more affordable=
 and compact, the technology becomes attractive for even small-scale labora=
tories.</td></tr><tr><th valign=3D"top" style=3D"width: 40%; font-size: 12p=
x;text-align: left;text-transform: uppercase;color: #777777;padding-top: 15=
px;padding-bottom: 15px;border-bottom: 1px solid #eeeeee;font-family: Helve=
tica, Arial, sans-serif;">Aim</th><td valign=3D"top" style=3D"-webkit-text-=
size-adjust: 100%;-ms-text-size-adjust: 100%;mso-table-lspace: 0pt;mso-tabl=
e-rspace: 0pt;text-align:left;padding-top: 15px;padding-bottom: 15px;border=
-bottom: 1px solid #eeeeee;font-family: Helvetica, Arial, sans-serif;">The =
main aim of this presentation is to give the audience a basic understanding=
 of Metagenomics as a new technology for pathogen detection. It highlights =
its basics in bio informatics and explains the overall processing steps in =
metagenomics. <br><br>The presentation explains the IT processing chain req=
uired to determine hits for pathogens. The author has expert knowledge with=
 regard to IT processing for metagenomics as he has developed a highly accu=
rate and efficient software system for this very purpose.<br><br>The presen=
tation covers recent scientific publications where metagenomics was success=
fully applied to detect tick-borne infections in ticks, dogs and deer based=
 on inexpensive sequencing devices. We show how respective results for DNA =
matches can be reproduced easily via user-friendly software on the basis of=
 raw DNA sequencing files.<br><br>The presentation further details metageno=
mics for pathogen detection via three experiments conducted by the author a=
nd co-researchers: <br>=E2=80=A2	Software based analysis results are shown =
based on raw sequencing files from 8 field-collected ticks. Some major tick=
-borne infections were discovered. <br>=E2=80=A2	Software based analysis re=
sults are shown based on raw sequencing files from 15 human saliva samples =
to find typical human viruses often occurring in the mouth. <br>=E2=80=A2	2=
0 soil samples were sequenced to detect fungi which are harmful to certain =
plants in soil using Nanopore sequencing. Results for one fungus were confi=
rmed with a PCR test.<br><br>More generally, the three experiments aim to s=
how the broad use of Metagenomics in various fields such as ecological surv=
eillance, medicine, and agriculture.</td></tr><tr><th valign=3D"top" style=
