ize-adjust: 100%;mso-table-lspace: 0pt;mso-table-rspace: 0pt;text-align:lef=
t;padding-top: 15px;padding-bottom: 15px;border-bottom: 1px solid #eeeeee;f=
ont-family: Helvetica, Arial, sans-serif;">As mentioned in =E2=80=9CAims=E2=
=80=9D the presentation covers three experiments:<br>1)	For a small study, =
we received the raw genomic data files from 10 humans who had their genome =
analyzed via =E2=80=9CDante Labs=E2=80=9D (an online service for personaliz=
ed whole genome sequencing). The sequencing was based on saliva samples. Th=
e data owners gave consent to the analysis prior to the experiment. We anal=
yzed the raw data files with respect to DNA fragments from human viruses us=
ing metagenomics software (named =E2=80=9CGenestrip=E2=80=9D). Our analysis=
 database covers 750 genomes of most to all viruses and virus variants that=
 are currently known to be potentially pathogenic to humans (to be inspecte=
d online via <br>https://genestrip.it.hs-heilbronn.de/gweb/dbinfo?lan=3Den&=
amp;dbid=3D5<br>. We checked respective hit counts and statistical results =
for consistency and checked whether strong hits aligned with general medica=
l expectations for viruses in the mouth.<br>2)	Regarding bacterial tick-bor=
ne infections, we used the raw analysis files from the Nature publication =
=E2=80=9CMetagenomic surveillance for bacterial tick-borne pathogens using =
nanopore adaptive sampling=E2=80=9D and processed them with our metagenomic=
s software. (The original raw data files are available in fastq format for =
download at the =E2=80=9CNational Center for Biotechnology Information=E2=
=80=9D in the US.) Eight field-collected ticks (species I. scapularis) from=
 different locations in the US were used for sequencing. We compared the me=
tagenomic analysis results of our software with those reported in the above=
 publication. Our analysis database covers over 700 genomes of potentially =
tick-borne bacterial pathogens (to be inspected online via <br>https://gene=
strip.it.hs-heilbronn.de/gweb/dbinfo?lan=3Den&amp;dbid=3D4<br>) including v=
arious species and strains of Yersinia, Bartonella, Rickettsia, Anaplasma, =
Ehrlichia, Borrelia, Borreliella, Chlamydia and Mycoplasma.<br>3)	We applie=
d metagenomics to check whether certain fungi were present in twenty soil s=
amples from around Heilbronn =E2=80=93 a German city known for growing wine=
. All of the ten fungi species tested are harmful to grape vine plants. The=
 analysis database can be inspected online via<br>https://genestrip.it.hs-h=
eilbronn.de/gweb/dbinfo?lan=3Den&amp;dbid=3D8<br>. <br>We used a Nanopore M=
inION as a sequencing device and a DNA extraction toolkit from Qiagen suita=
ble for soil (DNeasy PowerSoil Pro Kit). We followed the protocols from Qia=
gen and Nanopore for sample preparation. For partial control, the samples w=
ere tested against one fungus (=E2=80=9CBotrytis cinerea=E2=80=9D) via PCR =
with two samples being positive. (This fungus was also included in the meta=
genomic analysis.) <br>Experiment 3) was lead by Dr. Robert Friedrich and c=
onducted in cooperation with the Biolab of the experimenta Science Center i=
