px;border-bottom: 1px solid #eeeeee;font-family: Helvetica, Arial, sans-ser=
if;">Regarding the three experiments:<br>1)	There were high numbers of k-me=
rs for Human betaherpesvirus 7 in all 10 samples. As an example, consider =
=E2=80=9Cperson 3=E2=80=9C: We found Human betaherpesvirus 7 (with 448890 k=
-mers / 108664 unique k-mers) accounting for more than 78% of the viral gen=
ome. Note that k-mers represent unique DNA fragments from the respective pa=
thogen=E2=80=99s genome, each consisting of exactly 31 base pairs. The tota=
l number of k-mers in the corresponding two fastq files of person 3 was 297=
412346832, which corresponds to almost 200 GBs of compressed data on disk. =
In this case, the software-based analysis took 173 minutes on a modern Mac =
and computer memory consumption was less than 4 GB.<br>In addition, we foun=
d high numbers for Human gammaherpesvirus 4 (EBV) in the sample from person=
 5 (with 4061786 k-mers / 4151610 unique k-mers) but not in samples from an=
y persons. Person 5 had fatigue and other symptoms consistent with a potent=
ially chronic activation of EBV. The k-mers found there, cover more than 86=
% of the EBV genome.<br>Results for other viruses were inconclusive or k-me=
rs were too low to be considered hits.<br>2)	All of the results from the me=
ntioned publication could be recovered and confirmed. As an example, consid=
er =E2=80=9Ctick 3=E2=80=9D: We found Borrelia miyamotoi species (with 9599=
8 k-mers / 92786 unique k-mers), Borreliella burgdorferi species (with 1133=
0 k-mers / 11127 unique k-mers), Anaplasma phagocytophilum species (with 62=
53 k-mers / 3099 unique k-mers) and Rickettsia genus (with 7803 k-mers / 77=
96 unique k-mers). The total number of k-mers in the corresponding fastq fi=
le was 214436633. The above numbers constitute very good hits because of hi=
gh values and a consistent ratio between k-mers and unique k-mers. Analysis=
 time per fastq file was less than 2 minutes and computer memory consumptio=
n for analysis was less than 16 GB. Here is the direct online link to our r=
esults for tick 3:<br>https://genestrip.it.hs-heilbronn.de/gweb/view?lan=3D=
en&amp;jobid=3D35<br>3)	We found increased k-mers and unique k-mers for Bot=
rytis cinerea in just the samples where the PCR for Botrytis cinerea was po=
sitive. (Further details on this will be included in the presentation.)</td=
