, sans-serif;">Conclusions or Discussion</th><td valign=3D"top" style=3D"-w=
ebkit-text-size-adjust: 100%;-ms-text-size-adjust: 100%;mso-table-lspace: 0=
pt;mso-table-rspace: 0pt;text-align:left;padding-top: 15px;padding-bottom: =
15px;border-bottom: 1px solid #eeeeee;font-family: Helvetica, Arial, sans-s=
erif;">Experiment 1) shows that metagenomics can be used to find viruses re=
siding in the mouth. The samples confirmed the presence of Human betaherpes=
virus 7 in each of the individuals. This is impressive when considering tha=
t the actual sequencing data was not even gathered for the purpose of patho=
gen detection but for the analysis of the person=E2=80=99s (human) genome i=
nstead. Human betaherpesvirus 7 has a prevalence rate greater than 85%, whi=
ch is in line with the results for our samples. It resides in saliva glands=
 which also matches our type samples.<br>The hit for the EBV in the case of=
 person 5 was indicative of an active EBV infection and matched the person=
=E2=80=99s overall symptoms.<br><br>Experiment 2) confirmed the results of =
a Nature publication that analyzed bacterial pathogens in field-collected t=
icks. Our software analysis on the raw data files was perfectly consistent =
with the findings from the publication. This partly confirms the accuracy o=
f the employed software but more importantly, it confirms that metagenomics=
 would be an appropriate tool for tick surveillance. The results are sugges=
tive for applying this technology to ticks collected after a tick bite: by =
taking a related future test, a person bitten by a tick could be informed a=
bout the tick=E2=80=99s pathogens and could take appropriate actions to pre=
vent potential disease development.<br><br>Experiment 3) suggests that Meta=
genomics can potentially be used in agriculture to detect fungal infections=
 and to predict or prevent an outbreak in vineyards, for example. We mainly=
 chose the example to gain experience with sequencing and analysis tools wi=
thout (already) having to deal with human samples or samples potentially da=
ngerous to humans. The device that we used is small and relatively easy to =
handle. Experiment 3) also allowed for assessing the total cost for a poten=
tial future test (i.e. for a =E2=80=9Ctick test=E2=80=9D as mentioned above=
): We used an inexpensive sequencing device which was also used in the abov=
e-mentioned publication (worth 2,000 USD). Associated consumable materials =
per test are worth less than 100 USD. The staff hours for a future test are=
 high as it takes approximately one work day to prepare a respective sample=
 for sequencing. However, by using an approach called multiplexing, about 2=
4 samples can be jointly prepared and sequenced, which may greatly reduce s=
taff hours per sample. The cost and time for the up-following IT-based anal=
ysis is negligible.</td></tr><tr><th valign=3D"top" style=3D"width: 40%; fo=
