ust: 100%;-ms-text-size-adjust: 100%;mso-table-lspace: 0pt;mso-table-rspace=
: 0pt;text-align:left;padding-top: 15px;padding-bottom: 15px;border-bottom:=
 1px solid #eeeeee;font-family: Helvetica, Arial, sans-serif;">The few exis=
ting publications on metagenomics for pathogen detection mainly focus on in=
fections in animals. In this context, metagenomics has been successfully ap=
plied to detect blood-borne chronic infections including Mycoplasma, Barton=
ella, Babesia and types of Filaria. Borrelia has also been successfully det=
ected in ticks via metagenomics. The pathogens can usually be identified do=
wn to the species level and beyond. Respective publications report that the=
 sensitivity of metagenomics is comparable to PCR if not better.<br>The aut=
hors=E2=80=99 experience and findings as presented via three experiments ar=
e in line with the findings from the above-mentioned scientific publication=
s.<br>Metagenomics is becoming a standard tool in various fields of science=
, in particular biology and ecology, as well as in research on veterinary m=
edicine.</td></tr><tr><th valign=3D"top" style=3D"width: 40%; font-size: 12=
px;text-align: left;text-transform: uppercase;color: #777777;padding-top: 1=
