-text-size-adjust: 100%;mso-table-lspace: 0pt;mso-table-rspace: 0pt;text-al=
ign:left;padding-top: 15px;padding-bottom: 15px;border-bottom: 1px solid #e=
eeeee;font-family: Helvetica, Arial, sans-serif;">The results of the presen=
tation and related publications show that metagenomics is on the verge of b=
ecoming an important diagnostic tool. Indeed, there already exist respectiv=
e lab tests from startup companies / labs. Some US hospitals use metagenomi=
c analysis to determine sepsis pathogens accurately and quickly for the bes=
t treatment outcome.<br><br>When metagenomics becomes broadly available for=
 diagnostics, physicians will not have to make as many upfront guesses on i=
nfections present in order to choose an appropriate test as metagenomics ca=
n cover many pathogens in a single test. Physicians and laboratory staff wi=
ll have to consider and integrate an IT based analysis step into the whole =
testing procedure. This differs from conventional diagnostic tests that do =
not demand much IT processing.<br><br>As metagenomics returns statistics on=
 DNA-based hits for pathogens, physicians will need training to read, under=
stand and interpret these statistics correctly for appropriate conclusions =
and therapeutic steps.<br><br>As the cost for metagenomic analysis has decr=
eased substantially, it has become attractive as an option to replace estab=
lished laboratory tests for pathogens.</td></tr><tr><th valign=3D"top" styl=
